{
  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: progressive left arm numbness/tingling with functional weakness and intermittent numbness extending from elbow to hand; accompanying neck pain; transient left-eye visual disturbance reported previously. - Symptom details: onset of left-arm numbness about two weeks ago during boxing class, gradually worsening; numbness involves elbow, arm, and some fingers; tingling in fingertips; occasional weakness affecting grip (difficulty holding objects, piano); pain not primary; resting or massage/hot baths did not help. Visual symptom: transient partial loss of peripheral vision in the left eye for about 20 minutes. Fatigue and occasional headaches noted. - Aggravating/relieving factors: boxing activity possibly contributing; rest does not improve numbness; no relief with massage or warm baths. - Past medical history: recent heavy cold/flu about a month ago; no major chronic medical problems reported; uses ibuprofen for muscle soreness; takes vitamin D; iron supplements sporadically (GP advised); sometimes Melatonin. - Medications/allergies: ibuprofen, vitamin D, iron supplements (infrequently), Melatonin; no reported drug allergies. - Family/social history: father with hypothyroidism and autoimmune issues; mother with type 2 diabetes, hypertension, hyperlipidemia; no stated neurological family history. Social: active with boxing; alcohol use around a month ago (uncharacteristic heavy drinking). - Vital signs/exam findings: not provided in transcript. - Labs/imaging: doctor plans an urgent neurology referral and brain imaging (MRI) as part of work-up; no labs/imaging results yet mentioned. - Red flags noted by clinician: transient vision change, unilateral limb symptoms with weakness, neck pain, and activity-related onset prompting concern for central nervous system/ demyelinating or vascular etiologies; discussion of MS as a possibility and need for MRI. ",
  "step2_vindicate": {
